### Why is "just add more instances" insufficient?

Horizontal scaling works when the constraint is CPU or memory in the stateless application layer. But when the bottleneck is a shared downstream resource, adding instances adds more clients competing for the same scarce thing.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

public class WrongLever {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Stateless bottleneck: CPU at 95% → add instances → CORRECT");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Shared DB bottleneck: DB connections at 100% → add instances");
        System.out.println("  → more instances open MORE connections → exhausts limit FASTER");
        System.out.println("  → WRONG lever, makes it WORSE");
    }
}
```

```text
Stateless bottleneck: CPU at 95% → add instances → CORRECT

Shared DB bottleneck: DB connections at 100% → add instances
  → more instances open MORE connections → exhausts limit FASTER
  → WRONG lever, makes it WORSE
```

### How do you fix a shared database bottleneck?

A connection proxy like PgBouncer multiplexes a bounded number of real connections. Per-tenant query concurrency limits prevent one tenant from monopolizing them.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

public class DbBottleneckFix {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int appInstances = 100;
        int connectionsPerInstance = 10;
        int totalWithoutProxy = appInstances * connectionsPerInstance;
        int proxyLimit = 50;

        System.out.println("Without proxy: " + totalWithoutProxy + " connections (exceeds DB limit)");
        System.out.println("With PgBouncer proxy: " + proxyLimit + " real connections (bounded)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Plus per-tenant query concurrency:");
        System.out.println("  BigCorp: max 5 concurrent queries");
        System.out.println("  Cannot hold all 50 connections");
    }
}
```

```text
Without proxy: 1000 connections (exceeds DB limit)
With PgBouncer proxy: 50 real connections (bounded)

Plus per-tenant query concurrency:
  BigCorp: max 5 concurrent queries
  Cannot hold all 50 connections
```

### How do you fix a third-party API quota bottleneck?

A shared distributed rate limiter enforces per-tenant sub-quotas so each tenant gets a fair slice of the total external budget.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

public class ExternalQuotaFix {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int totalQuota = 1000; // calls per second from payment gateway
        int tenants = 100;
        int perTenantQuota = totalQuota / tenants;

        System.out.println("External API quota: " + totalQuota + "/sec (shared)");
        System.out.println("Per-tenant sub-quota: " + perTenantQuota + "/sec");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("BigCorp cannot consume more than " + perTenantQuota + "/sec");
        System.out.println("Remaining " + (totalQuota - perTenantQuota)
                + "/sec available for all other tenants");
    }
}
```

```text
External API quota: 1000/sec (shared)
Per-tenant sub-quota: 10/sec

BigCorp cannot consume more than 10/sec
Remaining 990/sec available for all other tenants
```

### How do you fix a hot Kafka partition?

Use a composite partition key that spreads a large customer's events across many partitions while preserving finer-grained ordering.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

public class HotPartitionFix {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: key = customerId → all BigCorp events on ONE partition
        String wrongKey = "bigcorp";
        System.out.println("Wrong key: '" + wrongKey + "' → all events on partition "
                + Math.abs(wrongKey.hashCode() % 10));

        // CORRECT: composite key spreads across partitions
        String[] accounts = {"bigcorp:ACC001", "bigcorp:ACC002", "bigcorp:ACC003"};
        for (String key : accounts) {
            System.out.println("Composite key: '" + key + "' → partition "
                    + Math.abs(key.hashCode() % 10));
        }
        System.out.println("BigCorp's accounts now span multiple partitions and consumers");
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong key: 'bigcorp' → all events on partition 2
Composite key: 'bigcorp:ACC001' → partition 7
Composite key: 'bigcorp:ACC002' → partition 4
Composite key: 'bigcorp:ACC003' → partition 1
BigCorp's accounts now span multiple partitions and consumers
```

### How do you diagnose the real bottleneck?

The telltale sign is low instance CPU and memory but high latency and errors. The instances are waiting on something shared downstream.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

public class BottleneckDiagnosis {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Instance CPU: 15% (LOW)");
        System.out.println("Instance memory: 40% (LOW)");
        System.out.println("P99 latency: 5000ms (HIGH)");
        System.out.println("Error rate: 25% (HIGH)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Diagnosis: instances are idle, waiting on shared downstream resource");
        System.out.println("Adding instances will NOT help (wrong lever)");
        System.out.println("Fix: identify and bound the shared resource");
    }
}
```

```text
Instance CPU: 15% (LOW)
Instance memory: 40% (LOW)
P99 latency: 5000ms (HIGH)
Error rate: 25% (HIGH)

Diagnosis: instances are idle, waiting on shared downstream resource
Adding instances will NOT help (wrong lever)
Fix: identify and bound the shared resource
```

### How do you compare the three bottlenecks?

| Bottleneck | Why More Instances Fails | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Shared database | More connections exhaust DB limit | PgBouncer + per-tenant query concurrency + sharding |
| Third-party API quota | More instances race for fixed quota | Distributed per-tenant sub-quotas |
| Hot Kafka partition | One partition = one consumer, extras idle | Composite key to spread across partitions |

### What are common mistakes?

Reflexively adding instances without diagnosing which resource is actually saturated.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

// WRONG: "Latency is high? Scale out!"
// Adds 10 instances → 10 more DB connections → DB connection limit hit → WORSE

// CORRECT: "Instance CPU is low but latency is high → bottleneck is downstream"
// Fix the downstream resource: proxy, quota, partition key
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you implement a distributed per-tenant rate limiter with Redis?
- What happens when a Kafka partition rebalance occurs after adding partitions?
- How do you decide when to shard the database versus adding read replicas?
- What metrics tell you the difference between a stateless and a shared-resource bottleneck?
